<body>

<h1>ArgusNet &mdash; Functions in the App</h1>
<p class="subtitle">A description of every function currently available inside the ArgusNet application.</p>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">1. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Add a Target</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The user can add any device they want to monitor by entering its name and IP address (or hostname). A form opens in a popup window and after saving, the device appears immediately in the dashboard table.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">2. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Edit a Target</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The user can update the name or IP address of any existing target. The edit form opens pre-filled with the current values so the user only changes what they need.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">3. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Delete a Target</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The user can remove a target from the monitoring list. When deleted, the device and all its ping history are permanently removed from the database.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">4. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Ping a Single Target (Manual)</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">Each device in the table has a Ping button. Clicking it immediately sends a ping to that specific device and updates the row with the new status (Online / Offline), response time, and packet loss percentage in real time.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">5. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Ping All Targets at Once</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">A &ldquo;Ping All&rdquo; button at the top of the dashboard sends a ping to every device on the list in one click. All rows update at the same time when the results come back.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">6. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Auto-Refresh (Automatic Polling)</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The user can turn on automatic polling so the app pings all devices on its own without any manual action. The user chooses the interval (30 seconds, 1 minute, 2 minutes, 5 minutes, or 10 minutes). A countdown timer shows how many seconds are left until the next automatic ping. The setting is saved so it stays on even after closing and reopening the browser.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">7. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">View Device Status</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">Every device in the table shows its current status as a colored badge &mdash; green for Online and red for Offline. The status is based on the most recent ping result.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">8. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">View Response Time (Latency)</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The table shows two latency values for each device: the latency of the last ping in milliseconds, and the average latency calculated from all historical pings.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">9. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">View Uptime Percentage</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">For each device, the app calculates what percentage of all pings were successful and displays it as a number with a color-coded progress bar. Green means 99% or above, yellow means 90&ndash;99%, and red means below 90%.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">10. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">View Packet Loss Percentage</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The table shows the percentage of pings that failed (no reply received) for each device. This helps identify unstable connections even when the device appears mostly online.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">11. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Dashboard Summary Stats</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">Four summary cards at the top of the dashboard give an instant overview of the entire network: total number of targets being monitored, how many are currently Online, how many are Offline, and the average response time across all online devices.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">12. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Response Time Chart</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">Each device has a Chart button that opens a popup with a line chart showing the response time for the last 60 pings. Red dots on the chart mark moments when the device was offline. A dashed line shows the average latency as a reference. Hovering over any point on the chart shows the exact time and latency value in a tooltip.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">13. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Chart Mini Stats (Inside the Chart)</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">Inside the response time chart popup, four small stat boxes show a quick summary for that specific device: Uptime %, average latency, minimum latency recorded, and maximum latency recorded.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">14. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">View Full Ping History</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">A dedicated History page shows a complete log of every ping ever performed, with the device name, IP address, result (Online / Offline), response time, any error message, and the exact date and time of each ping.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">15. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Filter Ping History by Device</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">On the History page, the user can select a specific device from a dropdown to see only the ping history for that device instead of viewing the history of all devices mixed together.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">16. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">History Pagination</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">The history log is split into pages of 50 results each. The user can navigate between pages using Previous and Next buttons. A counter shows the current page, total pages, and total number of records.</p>
</div>

<div class="func-block">
  <p><span class="func-num">17. &nbsp;</span><span class="func-title">Navigate Between Pages</span></p>
  <p class="func-desc">A navigation bar at the top of the screen lets the user switch between the Dashboard and the History page. Switching pages is instant with no full browser reload. The auto-refresh countdown timer keeps running in the background even when the user is on the History page.</p>
</div>

<p class="footer">ArgusNet &mdash; 17 functions currently in the application &bull; May 2026</p>

</body>
</html>
